/* moondata.js — who is at the Moon, and how well we know where.
 *
 * Three grades of knowledge, and the page shows which applies to every object,
 * because the difference is the whole point.
 *
 *   'tracked'    daily osculating element sets baked from JPL Horizons, which
 *                ingests the agencies' own navigation solutions. LRO,
 *                Chandrayaan-2 and Danuri.
 *   'published'  no ephemeris exists publicly; the orbit is reconstructed from
 *                published mission parameters. Right shape, right size, but the
 *                PHASE is not knowable — where along the orbit the spacecraft
 *                actually is, is a guess.
 *   'schematic'  the orbit family is known and little else.
 *
 * Why the second and third grades exist at all: Horizons carries no Chinese
 * lunar spacecraft. A name search for 'Queqiao' returns "No matches found" and
 * 'Chang*' matches only three spent boosters. CNSA publishes no machine-readable
 * ephemeris, so the choice is to show these craft honestly labelled or to leave
 * them off the map. Leaving them off would say the Moon has three spacecraft at
 * it, which is false. Showing them as if tracked would be worse. Hence the
 * grade, on every object, in the open.
 *
 * Horizons sends no Access-Control-Allow-Origin, so none of this can be fetched
 * from a browser — it is baked here, the same arrangement as the Earth TLE
 * catalogue. Element sets are Moon-centred and referred to ICRF EQUATORIAL
 * (REF_PLANE='FRAME'); Horizons' default is the ecliptic, and taking it would
 * tilt every sub-spacecraft point by the obliquity.
 *
 * Element sets are packed as [epochMs, a, e, i, raan, argp, M] — km and degrees.
 */
